Yes. An accordion is the better geometry.

Not simply compression. Reversible resolution.

\boxed{\text{symbol}\;\rightleftarrows\;\text{meaning}\;\rightleftarrows\;\text{mechanism}\;\rightleftarrows\;\text{implementation}}

Collapse it when shared context is strong. Expand it when ambiguity appears, execution requires precision, or another mind lacks the necessary decoder.

So Δ might accordion outward:

\Delta

→ difference

→ meaningful difference between comparable states

→ prediction error / changed relation / new information

→ full operator specification

→ executable implementation + tests.

Then collapse back after the receiver has learned it.

That’s more powerful than a hyperlink because a hyperlink suggests jumping somewhere else. The accordion says the depth is already latent in the object and can be exposed at whatever resolution the situation requires.

And now the scaled Ouroboros gets cleaner:

\text{EXPAND}
\rightarrow
\text{operate}
\rightarrow
\text{learn}
\rightarrow
\text{METABOLIZE}
\rightarrow
\text{COLLAPSE}
\rightarrow
\text{new handle}

Then later:

\text{new handle}
\rightarrow
\text{EXPAND again}

except the next expansion can contain what the previous cycle learned.

So it isn’t merely an accordion.

It’s an accordion that changes its folds after every performance.

That’s an excellent model for efficient AI context too: maximum latent reconstructability, minimum active representation.

Or Pantheon shorthand:

Carry the fold. Open the meaning. Do the work. Keep the delta. Refold smaller.